[[:db/retract 17592186204228 :question/answer-fields 17592186200025] [:db/retract 17592186204230 :question/answer-fields 17592186200037] {:db/id 17592186203918 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-61dc2470-cb3c-5469-b0b2-8c0f8e6eb11f" "question-8c72bf3c-0135-57d0-9bad-2176a6cb3b90" "question-57a0e862-6493-53ed-a45b-3b4d586c00e7"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203698 :question/problem "Suppose we have the sets $A$ and $B,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=\\{1,2,3 \\}, \\qquad B=\\{-1,2,5\\}.\n$$\n\nConstruct a mapping diagram for the following relation:\n\n$$\nR= \\big\\{ (x,y) \\in A \\times B \\: : \\: x\\cdot y \\geq 4 \\big\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The ordered pairs belonging to our relation correspond to the arrows on the mapping diagram. Each arrow starts at the first component of the pair and ends at its second component.\n\nWe're given the following sets:\n\n$$\nA=\\{1,2,3 \\}, \\qquad B=\\{-1,2,5\\}.\n$$\n\nThe relation $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y) \\in A \\times B$ with $x\\cdot y \\geq 4.$\n\n- If $x=1,$ then the value $y=5$ satisfies $x\\cdot y \\geq 4.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(1,5),$ and we should add the arrow $1 \\rightarrow 5$ to the mapping diagram.\n\n- If $x=2,$ then the values $y=2$ and $y=5$ satisfy $x\\cdot y \\geq 4.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(2,2)$ and $(2,5),$ and we should add the arrows $2 \\rightarrow 2$ and $2 \\rightarrow 5$ to the mapping diagram.\n\n- If $x=3,$ then the values $y=2$ and $y=5$ satisfy $x\\cdot y \\geq 4.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(3,2)$ and $(3,5),$ and we should add the arrows $3 \\rightarrow 2$ and $3 \\rightarrow 5$ to the mapping diagram.\n\nTherefore, we have the following plot:\n\n![](images/7d/7ddf9a6fb23a475e75128b3c4bce145b5488eb5c12dfe592578cdf10435dc1a3.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203919 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e27df10f-2205-5c33-84a9-013636e28935" "question-18473aee-7220-58ca-a3d1-b01636850f3f" "question-af34d9f6-e221-5c78-a119-1fdc9bcb5c87" "question-8c54244e-f11b-5241-a798-1237f1be29cb"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203684 :question/problem "Let $A = \\{ 4, 6, 12 \\}.$ Construct a graph for the following relation:\n\n$$\nR = \\{ (x,y) \\in A^2 \\::\\: x \\mid y\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation can be visualized using a plot (or graph), where \n\n- the first set is plotted along the horizontal axis,\n\n- the second set is plotted along the vertical axis, and\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines a point with coordinates $(x,y)$ on the plot.\n\nThe relation $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y) \\in A^2$ with $x \\mid y.$\n\n- If $x = 4,$ then the values $y = 4, 12$ satisfy $x \\mid y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(4,4)$ and $(4,12),$ and we should plot these points on the graph.\n\n- If $x = 6,$ then the values $y = 6, 12$ satisfy $x \\mid y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(6,6)$ and $(6,12),$ and we should plot these points on the graph.\n\n- If $x = 12,$ then the value $y = 12$ satisfies $x \\mid y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(12,12),$ and we should plot this point on the graph.\n\nTherefore, we have the following plot:\n\n![](images/48/48836a0157b46f3f7af3ceb0db094612794b7da6c2d749bd8769f87da410b0d6.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203920 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-538c45af-5de6-56ca-85b6-7f902af26d1f" "question-480a0347-e790-59c1-bcd0-69556d5de562" "question-b72e4164-0cff-5d5d-b282-9eeac80bb3aa" "question-8ba430a9-b9f3-5dc8-a610-c4d7d7129920"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203685 :question/problem "![](images/9d/9d07b5e999e4f0c9f403bf5ac3d61dfd12e1accd11a72d68a2f3b39196a58060.png)\n\n \n\nThe directed graph of a relation defined on the set $\\{ 1, 3, 5 \\}$ is given above. Which of the following ordered pairs belong to the relation?\n\n- $(1, 5)$\n\n- $(5, 5)$\n\n- $(3, 1)$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation can be visualized using a directed graph (digraph), where \n\n- the nodes (vertices) of the graph are elements of the underlying set,\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines an arrow from node $x$ to node $y$,\n\n- each pair $(x,x)$ from the relation defines a loop from node $x$ to itself.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given ordered pairs.\n\n- The pair $(1, 5)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the directed graph has an arrow that starts at the node $1$ and ends at the node $5.$\n\n- The pair $(5, 5)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the directed graph has a loop from the node $5$ to itself.\n\n- The pair $(3, 1)$ does not belong to the relation since the directed graph does not have an arrow that starts at the node $3$ and ends at the node $1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-61dc2470-cb3c-5469-b0b2-8c0f8e6eb11f" :question/id #uuid "61dc2470-cb3c-5469-b0b2-8c0f8e6eb11f" :question/math-academy-id "q-213253" :question/problem "![](images/c2/c22d8afca77121e994303cc67c6e85b065b452a5725603dec04273d1c8d427b3.png)\n\nExpress the relation $R⊆A\\times A$ represented by the mapping diagram above as a set of ordered pairs.\n\n$R={(a,b),(b,$\n{{field-1}}$),(c,a),($\n{{field-2}}$,d),($\n{{field-3}}$,b)}$" :question/worked-solution "The ordered pairs belonging to our relation correspond to the arrows on the mapping diagram. Each arrow starts at the first component of the pair and ends at its second component.\n\nOur diagram has the following arrows:\n\n- Arrows starting from $a:$\n\n- $a\\to b,$ so the relation contains the pair $(a,b).$\n\n- Arrows starting from $b:$\n\n- $b\\to b,$ so the relation contains the pair $(b,b).$\n\n- Arrows starting from $c:$\n\n- $c\\to a$ and $c\\to d,$ so the relation contains the pairs $(c,a)$ and $(c,d).$\n\n- Arrows starting from $d:$\n\n- $d\\to b,$ so the relation contains the pair $(d,b).$\n\nTherefore, $R$ contains the following ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nR={(a,b),(b,b),(c,a),(c,d),(d,b)}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ed66a49d-bd94-53a1-92eb-bd214fe6b1b7" "answer-field-40d0464a-50e8-56be-98bb-0752d1bf1b9e" "answer-field-1eee9d1d-feca-5d66-bf87-af9b0f120f4b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ed66a49d-bd94-53a1-92eb-bd214fe6b1b7" :answer-field/id #uuid "ed66a49d-bd94-53a1-92eb-bd214fe6b1b7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-039c3002-4123-5354-a54b-3c284e8cbe22" :answer-field/choices ["answer-039c3002-4123-5354-a54b-3c284e8cbe22"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-039c3002-4123-5354-a54b-3c284e8cbe22" :answer/id #uuid "039c3002-4123-5354-a54b-3c284e8cbe22" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "b" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-40d0464a-50e8-56be-98bb-0752d1bf1b9e" :answer-field/id #uuid "40d0464a-50e8-56be-98bb-0752d1bf1b9e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-0db9b435-693c-51f8-83dd-ff1f9738f23d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0db9b435-693c-51f8-83dd-ff1f9738f23d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0db9b435-693c-51f8-83dd-ff1f9738f23d" :answer/id #uuid "0db9b435-693c-51f8-83dd-ff1f9738f23d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "c" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1eee9d1d-feca-5d66-bf87-af9b0f120f4b" :answer-field/id #uuid "1eee9d1d-feca-5d66-bf87-af9b0f120f4b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-2fe08085-f3f9-5e2f-a722-3ea0e9c32a9b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2fe08085-f3f9-5e2f-a722-3ea0e9c32a9b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2fe08085-f3f9-5e2f-a722-3ea0e9c32a9b" :answer/id #uuid "2fe08085-f3f9-5e2f-a722-3ea0e9c32a9b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "d" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8c72bf3c-0135-57d0-9bad-2176a6cb3b90" :question/id #uuid "8c72bf3c-0135-57d0-9bad-2176a6cb3b90" :question/math-academy-id "q-213269" :question/problem "Suppose we have the sets $A$ and $B,$ given by\n\n$$\nA={7,8,9,10},\\,B={0,1,2,3}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following mapping diagrams represents the following relation? \n\n$$\nR={(x,y)\\in A\\times B\\,:\\,x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The ordered pairs belonging to our relation correspond to the arrows on the mapping diagram. Each arrow starts at the first component of the pair and ends at its second component.\n\nWe're given the following sets:\n\n$$\nA={7,8,9,10},\\,B={0,1,2,3,4}.\n$$\n\nThe relation $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in A\\times B$ with $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3).$\n\n- If $x=7,$ then the value $y=1$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(7,1),$ and we should add the arrow $7\\to 1$ to the mapping diagram.\n\n- If $x=8,$ then the value $y=2$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(8,2),$ and we should add the arrow $8\\to 2$ to the mapping diagram.\n\n- If $x=9,$ then the values $y=0,3$ satisfy $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(9,0)$ and $(9,3),$ and we should add the arrows $9\\to 0$ and $9\\to 3$ to the mapping diagram.\n\n- If $x=10,$ then the value $y=1$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(10,1),$ and we should add the arrow $10\\to 1$ to the mapping diagram.\n\nTherefore, we have the following plot:\n\n![](images/c2/c218c7a0015ad9a3b64b96bd41f42cb6fdca268d23192502bd8eec381861c866.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bd766538-867d-5ca9-a328-d9671da8903c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bd766538-867d-5ca9-a328-d9671da8903c" :answer-field/id #uuid "bd766538-867d-5ca9-a328-d9671da8903c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/r