resented by the mapping diagram above as a set of ordered pairs.\n\n$R={(w,w),(w,$\n{{field-1}}$),($\n{{field-2}}$,x),(z,$\n{{field-3}}$)}$" :question/worked-solution "The ordered pairs belonging to our relation correspond to the arrows on the mapping diagram. Each arrow starts at the first component of the pair and ends at its second component.\n\nOur diagram has the following arrows:\n\n- Arrows starting from $w:$\n\n- $w\\to w$ and $w\\to z,$ so the relation contains the pairs $(w,w)$ and $(w,z).$\n\n- No arrows start from $x.$\n\n- Arrows starting from $y:$\n\n- $y\\to x,$ so the relation contains the pair $(y,x).$\n\n- Arrows starting from $z:$\n\n- $z\\to x,$ so the relation contains the pair $(z,x).$\n\nTherefore, $R$ contains the following ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nR={(w,w),(w,z),(y,x),(z,x)}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6247a186-33d8-5bdd-abab-82aa8c37f29b" "answer-field-5dcbbc5c-d4a7-5b48-9988-d808410582cc" "answer-field-c8604724-b466-5c49-9eac-6df36c526cb1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6247a186-33d8-5bdd-abab-82aa8c37f29b" :answer-field/id #uuid "6247a186-33d8-5bdd-abab-82aa8c37f29b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-3e9c041f-47e8-5bb5-8991-9df243d3ae3e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3e9c041f-47e8-5bb5-8991-9df243d3ae3e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3e9c041f-47e8-5bb5-8991-9df243d3ae3e" :answer/id #uuid "3e9c041f-47e8-5bb5-8991-9df243d3ae3e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "z" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5dcbbc5c-d4a7-5b48-9988-d808410582cc" :answer-field/id #uuid "5dcbbc5c-d4a7-5b48-9988-d808410582cc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-61e01a20-35e1-5a7a-9d7e-41618423ad8d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-61e01a20-35e1-5a7a-9d7e-41618423ad8d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-61e01a20-35e1-5a7a-9d7e-41618423ad8d" :answer/id #uuid "61e01a20-35e1-5a7a-9d7e-41618423ad8d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c8604724-b466-5c49-9eac-6df36c526cb1" :answer-field/id #uuid "c8604724-b466-5c49-9eac-6df36c526cb1" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-6a835395-6491-59ff-aba2-7fdd3ccb16ae" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6a835395-6491-59ff-aba2-7fdd3ccb16ae"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6a835395-6491-59ff-aba2-7fdd3ccb16ae" :answer/id #uuid "6a835395-6491-59ff-aba2-7fdd3ccb16ae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186204228 :question/problem "![](images/01/015cb5b350ad2692c1371d20c367e6c01a81b981351ba33b61e6c95854862d63.png)\n\nThe plot of the binary relation $S,$ defined over the sets $X={2,3,4}$ and $Y={♣,♠},$ is given above. Which of the following ordered pairs belong to the relation?\n\n- $(2,♠)$\n- $(3,♣)$\n- $(4,♣)$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation can be visualized using a plot (or graph), where \n\n- the first set is plotted along the horizontal axis,\n\n- the second set is plotted along the vertical axis, and\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines a point with coordinates $(x,y)$ on the plot.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given ordered pairs.\n\n- The pair $(2,♠)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the plot contains a point with these coordinates.\n\n- The pair $(3,♣)$ does not belong to the relation since the plot does not contain a point with such coordinates.\n\n- The pair $(4,♣)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the plot contains a point with these coordinates.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1dc780e3-5a21-59ce-aa65-e72163175a31"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1dc780e3-5a21-59ce-aa65-e72163175a31" :answer-field/id #uuid "1dc780e3-5a21-59ce-aa65-e72163175a31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-29ccb1b7-2ca6-5dee-b540-bece0f9bdc86" :answer-field/choices ["answer-88b2d8a9-0488-5aa6-bb5a-cb908ed36229" "answer-29ccb1b7-2ca6-5dee-b540-bece0f9bdc86" "answer-9c646536-2075-5ae1-8df0-abb2e9284492" "answer-d66daf91-eceb-5f72-b909-5358356f4844" "answer-a89cc823-46c1-5241-b3c5-27d4cd4aae51"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-88b2d8a9-0488-5aa6-bb5a-cb908ed36229" :answer/id #uuid "88b2d8a9-0488-5aa6-bb5a-cb908ed36229" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-29ccb1b7-2ca6-5dee-b540-bece0f9bdc86" :answer/id #uuid "29ccb1b7-2ca6-5dee-b540-bece0f9bdc86" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9c646536-2075-5ae1-8df0-abb2e9284492" :answer/id #uuid "9c646536-2075-5ae1-8df0-abb2e9284492" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d66daf91-eceb-5f72-b909-5358356f4844" :answer/id #uuid "d66daf91-eceb-5f72-b909-5358356f4844" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a89cc823-46c1-5241-b3c5-27d4cd4aae51" :answer/id #uuid "a89cc823-46c1-5241-b3c5-27d4cd4aae51" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e27df10f-2205-5c33-84a9-013636e28935" :question/id #uuid "e27df10f-2205-5c33-84a9-013636e28935" :question/math-academy-id "q-213167" :question/problem "![](images/35/35756183a7b4bf08baffb00d1f35dcc370b0e58831554daafa58d8f3d4359ab6.png)\n\nThe plot of the binary relation $S,$ defined over the set $X={2,4,6}$ is given above. Which of the following ordered pairs belong to the relation?\n\n- $(2,2)$\n- $(4,4)$\n- $(6,2)$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation can be visualized using a plot (or graph), where \n\n- the first set is plotted along the horizontal axis,\n\n- the second set is plotted along the vertical axis, and\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines a point with coordinates $(x,y)$ on the plot.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given ordered pairs.\n\n- The pair $(2,2)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the plot contains a point with these coordinates.\n\n- The pair $(4,4)$ does not belong to the relation since the plot does not contain a point with such coordinates.\n\n- The pair $(6,2)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the plot contains a point with these coordinates.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ca8636af-9023-557c-9e61-5c98ec22e1da"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ca8636af-9023-557c-9e61-5c98ec22e1da" :answer-field/id #uuid "ca8636af-9023-557c-9e61-5c98ec22e1da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-38c8fc2e-e74c-5869-91a3-f1b2979621f3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-38c8fc2e-e74c-5869-91a3-f1b2979621f3" "answer-6905a4c0-8040-5b6a-87f7-803e19676be0" "answer-26c9b0be-0411-58d9-acc8-3ddc5b99d9ee" "answer-13272a5b-2de1-5012-9d4c-d0b6e9760b36" "answer-edbf0707-8642-500c-bb4f-336aa28e52ad"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-38c8fc2e-e74c-5869-91a3-f1b2979621f3" :answer/id #uuid "38c8fc2e-e74c-5869-91a3-f1b2979621f3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6905a4c0-8040-5b6a-87f7-803e19676be0" :answer/id #uuid "6905a4c0-8040-5b6a-87f7-803e19676be0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-26c9b0be-0411-58d9-acc8-3ddc5b99d9ee" :answer/id #uuid "26c9b0be-0411-58d9-acc8-3ddc5b99d9ee" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-13272a5b-2de1-5012-9d4c-d0b6e9760b36" :answer/id #uuid "13272a5b-2de1-5012-9d4c-d0b6e9760b36" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-edbf0707-8642-500c-bb4f-336aa28e52ad" :answer/id #uuid "edbf0707-8642-500c-bb4f-336aa28e52ad" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-18473aee-7220-58ca-a3d1-b01636850f3f" :question/id #uuid "18473aee-7220-58ca-a3d1-b01636850f3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-213180" :question/problem "Let $A={1,2,3}.$ Which of the following is the graph representing the relation $R={(x,y)\\in {A}^{2}\\,:\\,x+y=4}?$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation can be visualized using a plot (or graph), where \n\n- the first set is plotted along the horizontal axis,\n\n- the second set is plotted along the vertical axis, and\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines a point with coordinates $(x,y)$ on the plot.\n\nThe relation $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {A}^{2}$ with $x+y=4.$\n\n- If $x=1,$ then the value $y=3$ satisfies $x+y=4.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(1,3),$ and we should plot this point on the graph.\n\n- If $x=2,$ then the value $y=2$ satisfies $x+y=4.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(2,2),$ and we should plot this point on the graph.\n\n- If $x=3,$ then the value $y=1$ satisfies $x+y=4.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(3,1),$ and we should plot this point on the graph.\n\nTherefore, we have the following plot:\n\n![](images/3d/3dec67ac3a21ca9db5967f5e650fd79d6aaadab05c900db24b7a9669a872f2f2.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e4c18e7a-0cb9-5985-b003-006e3604014c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e4c18e7a-0cb9-5985-b003-006e3604014c" :answer-field/id #uuid "e4c18e7a-0cb9-5985-b003-006e3604014c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6291cf00-36f4-5f85-9343-de7d394117ce" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6291cf00-36f4-5f85-9343-de7d394117ce" "answer-da12c4e3-6b6f-543e-a71d-beed250594af" "answer-52a8bfcd-d6f1-5e95-a1b3-95f561da75f4" "answer-b4795e31-10f4-5cb3-b737-d0f8c02771c1" "answer-f7af45d2-f112-51b6-9f3b-2e3977b69236"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6291cf00-36f4-5f85-9343-de7d394117ce" :answer/id #uuid "6291cf00-36f4-5f85-9343-de7d394117ce" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/3d/3dec67ac3a21ca9db5967f5e650fd79d6aaadab05c900db24b7a9669a872f2f2.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-da12c4e3-6b6f-543e-a71d-beed250594af" :answer/id #uuid "da12c4e3-6b6f-543e-a71d-beed250594af" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/4d/4d08b577582567c0af86201b86ebd6b8a6a837cb0db91cbc047773f58017f3b8.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-52a8bfcd-d6f1-5e95-a1b3-95f561da75f4" :answer/id #uuid "52a8bfcd-d6f1-5e95-a1b3-95f561da75f4" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/59/59ada0e07a72956a300ac0de27e75ea8598d7f7d0ccc1e2b51fc31c127cf8f15.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b4795e31-10f4-5cb3-b737-d0f8c02771c1" :answer/id #uuid "b4795e31-10f4-5cb3-b737-d0f8c02771c1" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/6e/6e3abe1d717f07230d9712db4a5006b9da27859760d20df4eafe6b7b5f24afc3.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f7af45d2-f112-51b6-9f3b-2e3977b69236" :answer/id #uuid "f7af45d2-f112-51b6-9f3b-2e3977b69236" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/fb/fb730add8167fcad4b03902d9cafbb0163f3ae00ee7d4c635d9c1d82211c3162.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-af34d9f6-e221-5c78-a119-1fdc9bcb5c87" :question/id #uuid "af34d9f6-e221-5c78-a119-1fdc9bcb5c87" :question/math-academy-id "q-212114" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph representing the relation $R={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<81}?$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation can be visualized using a plot (or graph), where \n\n- the first set goes along the horizontal line,\n\n- the second set goes along the vertical line,\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines a point with coordinates $(x,y)$ on the plot.\n\nIn our case, the plot contains all the points in ${R}^{2}$ for which \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<81.\n$$\n\nThese are the points that lie *inside* the circle of radius $9$ centered at the origin.\n\nFinally, since we have a strict inequality, the points on the circle must be excluded from the plot, which we depict by using a *dashed* curve:\n\n![](images/4e/4e08efd6af5e38ed82c0c1659088620b673afbfad5a751c07a492dd0fc4c497c.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b5d5380e-ae3a-5c0f-ac14-bf33cf3c6bb1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b5d5380e-ae3a-5c0f-ac14-bf33cf3c6bb1" :answer-field/id #uuid "b5d5380e-ae3a-5c0f-ac14-bf33cf3c6bb1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-190f3569-4d1f-5902-8f57-3f9b901f5eb2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-dccf3751-5b92-5a35-99dd-3f4174a3fc82" "answer-190f3569-4d1f-5902-8f57-3f9b901f5eb2" "answer-0c6d623c-ebdd-5e72-8f57-388e3aa09b2c" "answer-6962413b-7c51-50e9-9538-19bbb06c7019" "answer-2b08bc91-d831-5725-b463-d06eeef20685"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dccf3751-5b92-5a35-99dd-3f4174a3fc82" :answer/id #uuid "dccf3751-5b92-5a35-99dd-3f4174a3fc82" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/14/1495bb2a62a1b20082862f0a547a7d89a91a2dd6238d1452a8a72800733558b7.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-190f3569-4d1f-5902-8f57-3f9b901f5eb2" :answer/id #uuid "190f3569-4d1f-5902-8f57-3f9b901f5eb2" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/4e/4e08efd6af5e38ed82c0c1659088620b673afbfad5a751c07a492dd0fc4c497c.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0c6d623c-ebdd-5e72-8f57-388e3aa09b2c" :answer/id #uuid "0c6d623c-ebdd-5e72-8f57-388e3aa09b2c" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/5e/5eb68dca550b17d13a752b44fcf6c026f58e266100210d31834a73b19f9ba6e9.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6962413b-7c51-50e9-9538-19bbb06c7019" :answer/id #uuid "6962413b-7c51-50e9-9538-19bbb06c7019" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/a6/a6c5137b47544ab46146ec0f951442c1cb268ec19b4312d9364647f4148c5fad.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2b08bc91-d831-5725-b463-d06eeef20685" :answer/id #uuid "2b08bc91-d831-5725-b463-d06eeef20685" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/d2/d23ceda71423d8ce91cf96f110df8705bf425c9b823450d70548d94612103aec.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8c54244e-f11b-5241-a798-1237f1be29cb" :question/id #uuid "8c54244e-f11b-5241-a798-1237f1be29cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-213188" :question/problem "Let $A={3,6,8}$ and $B={1,4,7}$ Which of the following is the graph representing the relation $R={(x,y)\\in A\\times B\\,:\\,x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)}?$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation can be visualized using a plot (or graph), where \n\n- the first set is plotted along the horizontal axis,\n\n- the second set is plotted along the vertical axis, and\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines a point with coordinates $(x,y)$ on the plot.\n\nThe relation $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in A\\times B$ with $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2).$\n\n- If $x=3,$ then the values $y=1,7$ satisfy $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(3,1)$ and $(3,7),$ and we should plot these points on the graph.\n\n- If $x=6,$ then the value $y=4$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(6,4),$ and we should plot this point on the graph.\n\n- If $x=8,$ then the value $y=4$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(8,4),$ and we should plot this point on the graph.\n\nTherefore, we have the following plot:\n\n![](images/42/42f68f95fe216faacbfaa051ec0a1a6cf75fe7e4598061e94f2ec6be87f9541f.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c0ba57a4-06ca-5eb1-846d-69ae94a3af23"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c0ba57a4-06ca-5eb1-846d-69ae94a3af23" :answer-field/id #uuid "c0ba57a4-06ca-5eb1-846d-69ae94a3af23" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1b552ec2-8bae-5f6c-b902-514499f8d3ef" :answer-field/choices ["answer-aa4f4494-0c2f-514c-a137-c3a3d314a308" "answer-1b552ec2-8bae-5f6c-b902-514499f8d3ef" "answer-8327d456-842b-5b16-abc5-17cbf70f9780" "answer-365f26d9-e9d3-55db-9fae-badc735ec9c6" "answer-474bc48d-3b12-51f8-837e-1c568bd96891"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aa4f4494-0c2f-514c-a137-c3a3d314a308" :answer/id #uuid "aa4f4494-0c2f-514c-a137-c3a3d314a308" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/1d/1dfcdd95669026e2263465f71b67d4913fdfddee18138a8516487ed14d3e02c1.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1b552ec2-8bae-5f6c-b902-514499f8d3ef" :answer/id #uuid "1b552ec2-8bae-5f6c-b902-514499f8d3ef" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/42/42f68f95fe216faacbfaa051ec0a1a6cf75fe7e4598061e94f2ec6be87f9541f.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8327d456-842b-5b16-abc5-17cbf70f9780" :answer/id #uuid "8327d456-842b-5b16-abc5-17cbf70f9780" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/53/53e4726a02da9ba18322717180334849b93ce1cc15c59dcd22fa3e97b6fe8cfb.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-365f26d9-e9d3-55db-9fae-badc735ec9c6" :answer/id #uuid "365f26d9-e9d3-55db-9fae-badc735ec9c6" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/56/56d64fc3b536fe0ef88e7ac600d98a269167fd2039f8b3bcce586ae2a9b72ef5.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-474bc48d-3b12-51f8-837e-1c568bd96891" :answer/id #uuid "474bc48d-3b12-51f8-837e-1c568bd96891" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/a4/a4d1ba20a0d170c62380b80a037c316ed894ceb0af624a0591bfabdf7a0a036b.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-538c45af-5de6-56ca-85b6-7f902af26d1f" :question/id #uuid "538c45af-5de6-56ca-85b6-7f902af26d1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-213261" :question/problem "![](images/de/ded4e8a94730585809c5534fee8d401481c919d529a67cd088e96b8797f2b237.png)\n\nThe directed graph of a relation defined on the set ${2,5,8}$ is given above. Which of the following ordered pairs belong to the relation?\n\n- $(2,2)$\n- $(2,8)$\n- $(8,5)$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation can be visualized using a directed graph (digraph), where \n\n- the nodes (vertices) of the graph are elements of the underlying set,\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines an arrow from node $x$ to node $y$,\n\n- each pair $(x,x)$ from the relation defines a loop from node $x$ to itself.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given ordered pairs.\n\n- The pair $(2,2)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the directed graph has a loop from the node $2$ to itself.\n\n- The pair $(2,8)$ does not belong to the relation since the directed graph does not have an arrow that starts at the node $2$ and ends at the node $8.$\n\n- The pair $(8,5)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the directed graph has an arrow that starts at the node $8$ and ends at the node $5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-16e4a9b9-ed3e-5143-9ca1-42dbee28c3b0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-16e4a9b9-ed3e-5143-9ca1-42dbee28c3b0" :answer-field/id #uuid "16e4a9b9-ed3e-5143-9ca1-42dbee28c3b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-229aefd2-11b3-5163-b906-bbd86d424316" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e8456360-6cf3-5dbc-9b03-efff334fe8ec" "answer-229aefd2-11b3-5163-b906-bbd86d424316" "answer-6bce8261-f995-5f77-9c0b-b77b795f8640" "answer-fd84b5df-a423-5a5d-ad6d-2ba9d4d34b30" "answer-781adc97-4ef9-50db-8150-4fb68c77d192"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e8456360-6cf3-5dbc-9b03-efff334fe8ec" :answer/id #uuid "e8456360-6cf3-5dbc-9b03-efff334fe8ec" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-229aefd2-11b3-5163-b906-bbd86d424316" :answer/id #uuid "229aefd2-11b3-5163-b906-bbd86d424316" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6bce8261-f995-5f77-9c0b-b77b795f8640" :answer/id #uuid "6bce8261-f995-5f77-9c0b-b77b795f8640" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fd84b5df-a423-5a5d-ad6d-2ba9d4d34b30" :answer/id #uuid "fd84b5df-a423-5a5d-ad6d-2ba9d4d34b30" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-781adc97-4ef9-50db-8150-4fb68c77d192" :answer/id #uuid "781adc97-4ef9-50db-8150-4fb68c77d192" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-480a0347-e790-59c1-bcd0-69556d5de562" :question/id #uuid "480a0347-e790-59c1-bcd0-69556d5de562" :question/math-academy-id "q-213265" :question/problem "Let $A={1,2,4}.$ Which of the following directed graphs represents the following relation? \n\n$$\nR={(x,y)\\in {A}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\le {x}^{2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation can be visualized using a directed graph (digraph), where \n\n- the nodes (vertices) of the graph are elements of the underlying set,\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines an arrow from node $x$ to node $y$,\n\n- each pair $(x,x)$ from the relation defines a loop from node $x$ to itself.\n\nIn our case, the nodes of the directed graph are marked by the elements of $A.$\n\n![](images/fe/fe1e7ad055ab47eca3234dfc3797a7316c554868a62cb4e46a303d369baeeaa0.png)\n\n \n\nThe relation $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {A}^{2}$ with $y\\le {x}^{2}.$\n\n- If $x=1,$ then the value $y=1$ satisfies $y\\le {x}^{2}.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(1,1),$ and we should add the following arrow to our directed graph:\n\n$$\n1\\to 1\n$$\n\n- If $x=2,$ then the values $y=1,2,4$ satisfy $y\\le {x}^{2}.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(2,1),\\,(2,2),\\,(2,4),$ and we should add the following arrows to our directed graph:\n\n$$\n2\\to 1,\\,2\\to 2,\\,2\\to 4\n$$\n\n- If $x=4,$ then the values $y=1,2,4$ satisfy $y\\le {x}^{2}.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(4,1),$ $(4,2),$ $(4,4),$ and we should add the following arrows to our directed graph:\n\n$$\n4\\to 1,\\,4\\to 2,\\,4\\to 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following digraph:\n\n![](images/e5/e5f595bac27c15ffd3adca91b9a20cefdda9abb18123d706fc50d0fc625f5454.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3780fa3c-bf58-5339-9cc7-3a9993d61a13"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3780fa3c-bf58-5339-9cc7-3a9993d61a13" :answer-field/id #uuid "3780fa3c-bf58-5339-9cc7-3a9993d61a13" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ee709334-bc56-57b5-87bc-4b39d37c191e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-52c5ec6a-04eb-55d2-b596-1ab172faa1b1" "answer-7f2ec819-24ad-5575-897b-3d71478af35f" "answer-05f6ce38-4ec7-5c4b-9a30-15cd62314ac4" "answer-0aca0654-5a0b-5d1a-9c1a-4a631fc7caaf" "answer-ee709334-bc56-57b5-87bc-4b39d37c191e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-52c5ec6a-04eb-55d2-b596-1ab172faa1b1" :answer/id #uuid "52c5ec6a-04eb-55d2-b596-1ab172faa1b1" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/2a/2a0b3b63880acd92b9a549f19b04fc24f14c5c133d6bed4dec23797df5ba4773.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7f2ec819-24ad-5575-897b-3d71478af35f" :answer/id #uuid "7f2ec819-24ad-5575-897b-3d71478af35f" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/5c/5c2794b26dd4e70b60e99a77703c5e117793b86dc2c68045620d1619cf6509a4.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-05f6ce38-4ec7-5c4b-9a30-15cd62314ac4" :answer/id #uuid "05f6ce38-4ec7-5c4b-9a30-15cd62314ac4" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/60/6048ff5e6150a98c1074716b6d58905171a9d074859b071b43d4795a841bca84.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0aca0654-5a0b-5d1a-9c1a-4a631fc7caaf" :answer/id #uuid "0aca0654-5a0b-5d1a-9c1a-4a631fc7caaf" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/d1/d1afa34c5eb553358d3c267cc5a38ed05d8e8566af2d650db714d0d5a9cdcb10.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ee709334-bc56-57b5-87bc-4b39d37c191e" :answer/id #uuid "ee709334-bc56-57b5-87bc-4b39d37c191e" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/e5/e5f595bac27c15ffd3adca91b9a20cefdda9abb18123d706fc50d0fc625f5454.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186204230 :question/problem "![](images/84/847bcee3b9f9fc6672d5858111866141e043e390605cd92fb5579fd5193d7df4.png)\n\nThe directed graph of a relation defined on the set ${1,2,3}$ is given above. Which of the following ordered pairs belong to the relation?\n\n- $(2,1)$\n- $(3,3)$\n- $(2,3)$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation can be visualized using a directed graph (digraph), where \n\n- the nodes (vertices) of the graph are elements of the underlying set,\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines an arrow from node $x$ to node $y$,\n\n- each pair $(x,x)$ from the relation defines a loop from node $x$ to itself.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given ordered pairs.\n\n- The pair $(2,1)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the directed graph has an arrow that starts at the node $2$ and ends at the node $1.$\n\n- The pair $(3,3)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the directed graph has a loop from the node $3$ to itself.\n\n- The pair $(2,3)$ does not belong to the relation since the directed graph does not have an arrow that starts at the node $2$ and ends at the node $3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-001f212a-2c89-570f-a93d-35b3d3dd68fe"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-001f212a-2c89-570f-a93d-35b3d3dd68fe" :answer-field/id #uuid "001f212a-2c89-570f-a93d-35b3d3dd68fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c8d15f20-fd7b-5b6b-a3b1-2360e322ea86" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c8d15f20-fd7b-5b6b-a3b1-2360e322ea86" "answer-0e527545-c8a4-5706-a526-a45e5bdba53b" "answer-aae26d10-6696-5509-93bb-3f2a97889821" "answer-bd0608ce-eb8d-5612-b223-1f6931919f8c" "answer-e877ff7a-c423-5005-80f4-b878e8065f4b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c8d15f20-fd7b-5b6b-a3b1-2360e322ea86" :answer/id #uuid "c8d15f20-fd7b-5b6b-a3b1-2360e322ea86" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0e527545-c8a4-5706-a526-a45e5bdba53b" :answer/id #uuid "0e527545-c8a4-5706-a526-a45e5bdba53b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aae26d10-6696-5509-93bb-3f2a97889821" :answer/id #uuid "aae26d10-6696-5509-93bb-3f2a97889821" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bd0608ce-eb8d-5612-b223-1f6931919f8c" :answer/id #uuid "bd0608ce-eb8d-5612-b223-1f6931919f8c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e877ff7a-c423-5005-80f4-b878e8065f4b" :answer/id #uuid "e877ff7a-c423-5005-80f4-b878e8065f4b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b72e4164-0cff-5d5d-b282-9eeac80bb3aa" :question/id #uuid "b72e4164-0cff-5d5d-b282-9eeac80bb3aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-213272" :question/problem "Let $A={Z,Q,R}.$ Which of the following directed graphs represents the following relation? \n\n$$\nR={(X,Y)\\in {A}^{2}\\,:\\,Y⊆X}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation can be visualized using a directed graph (digraph), where \n\n- the nodes (vertices) of the graph are elements of the underlying set,\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines an arrow from node $x$ to node $y$,\n\n- each pair $(x,x)$ from the relation defines a loop from node $x$ to itself.\n\nIn our case, the nodes of the directed graph are marked by the elements of $A.$\n\n![](images/98/983be4b904bbbf72f896c2e96d3691c74179c9e078494d581fb8c9b2c0c64b7f.png)\n\n \n\nThe relation $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(X,Y)\\in {A}^{2}$ with $Y⊂X.$\n\n- If $X=Z,$ then the value $Y=Z$ satisfies $Y⊆X.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(Z,Z),$ and we should add the following arrow  to our directed graph:\n\n$$\nZ\\to Z\n$$\n\n- If $X=Q,$ then the values $Y=Z,Q$ satisfy $Y⊆X.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(Q,Z),(Q,Q),$ and we should add the following arrows to our directed graph:\n\n$$\nQ\\to Z,\\,Q\\to Q\n$$\n\n- If $X=R,$ then the values $y=Z,Q,R$ satisfy $Y⊆X.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(R,Z),$ $(R,Q),$ $(R,R),$ and we should add the following arrows to our directed graph:\n\n$$\nR\\to Z,\\,R\\to Q,\\,R\\to R\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following digraph:\n\n![](images/74/74f2f01dd9b2672f933118f6cec0bc0b83b9a0fa94b0e75536a7db9f0fe34e6b.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-00feca98-0fa4-5498-b814-25e2c4cd6245"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-00feca98-0fa4-5498-b814-25e2c4cd6245" :answer-field/id #uuid "00feca98-0fa4-5498-b814-25e2c4cd6245" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e393cf06-a753-55ca-b724-8f861d35ce36" :answer-field/choices ["answer-107d4507-8231-5b8c-8e97-84b83cc2d7ba" "answer-b42a2495-1c67-5e03-997f-625dd59d55ce" "answer-3e31cd7c-7470-5588-9360-d669280cc5a7" "answer-e393cf06-a753-55ca-b724-8f861d35ce36" "answer-0ecde3ed-974d-55f9-b20d-3aefeefd4a9d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-107d4507-8231-5b8c-8e97-84b83cc2d7ba" :answer/id #uuid "107d4507-8231-5b8c-8e97-84b83cc2d7ba" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/0a/0a5edb58642893336967966154abdb816c60319743e3b826e5898798298f1bfd.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b42a2495-1c67-5e03-997f-625dd59d55ce" :answer/id #uuid "b42a2495-1c67-5e03-997f-625dd59d55ce" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/17/17bd7e9f3c66b92d036089640bc3b74d5996de9a498bd64f2262a54695e130b4.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3e31cd7c-7470-5588-9360-d669280cc5a7" :answer/id #uuid "3e31cd7c-7470-5588-9360-d669280cc5a7" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/47/4779a070a7801248e4fd9ea5e4361b2ae4bff82e5cf7aa372989b84b3eb92c92.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e393cf06-a753-55ca-b724-8f861d35ce36" :answer/id #uuid "e393cf06-a753-55ca-b724-8f861d35ce36" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/74/74f2f01dd9b2672f933118f6cec0bc0b83b9a0fa94b0e75536a7db9f0fe34e6b.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0ecde3ed-974d-55f9-b20d-3aefeefd4a9d" :answer/id #uuid "0ecde3ed-974d-55f9-b20d-3aefeefd4a9d" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/f8/f8abac994a863447da2182e86df34cbdbcf019d58c9a606f507403f15890947d.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8ba430a9-b9f3-5dc8-a610-c4d7d7129920" :question/id #uuid "8ba430a9-b9f3-5dc8-a610-c4d7d7129920" :question/math-academy-id "q-213262" :question/problem "![](images/4b/4b23d63f453246fb606969ba651104097fca785324d5d0fd2203e176dda19fa5.png)\n\nThe directed graph of a relation defined on the set ${\\alpha ,\\beta ,\\gamma }$ is given above. Which of the following ordered pairs belong to the relation?\n\n- $(\\beta ,\\gamma )$\n- $(\\gamma ,\\alpha )$\n- $(\\alpha ,\\beta )$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation can be visualized using a directed graph (digraph), where \n\n- the nodes (vertices) of the graph are elements of the underlying set,\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines an arrow from node $x$ to node $y$,\n\n- each pair $(x,x)$ from the relation defines a loop from node $x$ to itself.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given ordered pairs.\n\n- The pair  $(\\beta ,\\gamma )$ does not belong to the relation since the directed graph does not have an arrow that starts at the node $\\beta $ and ends at the node $\\gamma .$\n\n- The pair $(\\gamma ,\\alpha )$ does not belong to the relation since the directed graph does not have an arrow that starts at the node $\\gamma $ and ends at the node $\\alpha .$\n\n- The pair $(\\alpha ,\\beta )$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the directed graph has a an arrow that starts at the node $\\alpha $ and ends at the node $\\beta .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0bc10d4e-de6c-5af8-9374-56bcd531b20c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0bc10d4e-de6c-5af8-9374-56bcd531b20c" :answer-field/id #uuid "0bc10d4e-de6c-5af8-9374-56bcd531b20c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1997f0c1-dbf6-5676-9a65-8a59d13b359b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e84d09a8-9f4e-5918-a25a-eab0fa6840b0" "answer-cc702308-7c8f-52c1-8bba-64531db6e716" "answer-e38ff878-929d-5ee9-9b71-f303cbb883cc" "answer-4f900224-55b2-5719-9556-cfc3c22bf31d" "answer-1997f0c1-dbf6-5676-9a65-8a59d13b359b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e84d09a8-9f4e-5918-a25a-eab0fa6840b0" :answer/id #uuid "e84d09a8-9f4e-5918-a25a-eab0fa6840b0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cc702308-7c8f-52c1-8bba-64531db6e716" :answer/id #uuid "cc702308-7c8f-52c1-8bba-64531db6e716" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e38ff878-929d-5ee9-9b71-f303cbb883cc" :answer/id #uuid "e38ff878-929d-5ee9-9b71-f303cbb883cc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4f900224-55b2-5719-9556-cfc3c22bf31d" :answer/id #uuid "4f900224-55b2-5719-9556-cfc3c22bf31d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1997f0c1-dbf6-5676-9a65-8a59d13b359b" :answer/id #uuid "1997f0c1-dbf6-5676-9a65-8a59d13b359b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186205238 :tutorial/content "We've already seen how adjacency matrices describe finite relations. In this lesson, we'll learn other common ways to describe relations visually. \n\nSimilar to functions, binary relations can be visualized using **mapping diagrams**. \n\nFor example, consider the binary relation \n\n$$\nR = \\{ (1,4), (1,5), (2, 4), (3,5) \\},\n$$\n\ndefined on $A = \\{ 1, 2, 3 \\}$ and $B = \\{ 4, 5 \\}.$ \n\nThis relation corresponds to the diagram shown below, where the ordered pairs belonging to our relation correspond to the arrows on the mapping diagram.\nEach arrow starts at the first component of the pair and ends at its second component.\n\n![](images/0e/0e5e4e1a1fe57428c551263959956ad0139766d5607b4afa6d918a2f121fe629.png)\n\n \n\nThe mapping diagram representation is usually used only for finite binary relations with relatively small underlying sets." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186205232 :tutorial/content "Binary relations can be visualized using a **plot** (or **graph**). \n\nFor example, consider the binary relation $R$ defined over $A = \\{ 1, 2, 3 \\}$ and $B = \\{ 4, 5 \\},$ given by\n\n$$\nR = \\{ (1,4), (1,5), (2, 4), (3,5) \\},\n$$\n\nThe relation $R$ corresponds to the plot shown below, where \n\n- the elements from the first set are written along the horizontal axis,\n\n- the elements from the second set are written along the vertical axis, and\n\n- each pair $(x,y)\\in R$ defines a point with coordinates $(x,y)$ on the plot.\n\n![](images/ff/ff049d0c79f75a14721d02576a83b945e9cfa1485353ad30a6c77d3ac156f3e4.png)\n\n \n\nPlots usually work well for finite and infinite (discrete or continuous) relations.\n\n**Watch out!** A relation may have points that line on the same vertical line, like the points $(1,4)$ and $(1,5)$ in $R.$ In general, a relation is *not* a function. Functions are a special case of relations, where the points with the same first component but different second components are not allowed. We'll learn more about these special kinds of relations in future lessons." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186205239 :tutorial/content "Any binary relation that's defined over one set can be visualized using a **directed graph** (or **digraph**), where\n\n- the **nodes** ( or **vertices**) of the graph are elements of the underlying set,\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines an **arrow** from node $x$ to node $y$, and\n\n- each pair $(x,x)$ from the relation defines a **loop** from node $x$ to itself.\n\nFor example, consider the binary relation on $A = \\{ 1, 2, 3 \\}$ defined as \n\n$$\nR = \\{ (1,2), (1,3), (2,3), (3,3) \\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw a digraph corresponding to this relation. \n\nWe start by drawing three *nodes* corresponding to the elements of $A.$\n\n![](images/58/58e85e2eeb4a3d0382687b0d551379492504fb45ecdd93a4cb6885799a0f799d.png)\n\n \n\nThe locations of the nodes can be arbitrary. However, we should ensure that the diagram is easy to read.\n\nWe now go through the elements of $R$ to decide where to place *arrows* and *loops*:\n\n- $(1,2)\\in R,$ so we add the arrow $1 \\to 2$ to the digraph\n\n- $(1,3)\\in R,$ so we add the arrow $1 \\to 3$ to the digraph\n\n- $(2,3)\\in R,$ so we add the arrow $2 \\to 3$ to the digraph\n\n- $(3,3)\\in R,$ so we add the loop $3 \\to 3$ to the digraph\n\nTherefore, we have the following digraph:\n\n![](images/34/34297fa91d8eab0e076e3b4e22359189b64271741cf2624e8535921fb6f8b302.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186204120 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
